{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon Route 53 Resolver Query Logging now available in Asia Pacific (New Zealand) - AWS","context":"Amazon Route 53 Resolver Query Logging is now available in the Asia Pacific (New Zealand) region. This feature allows logging of DNS queries originating from Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) resources. With query logging enabled, users can view queried domain names, AWS resources' source IP and instance ID, and received responses. Route 53 Resolver, the Amazon-provided DNS server in all VPCs, responds to queries for public DNS records, VPC-specific DNS names, and private hosted zones. Query logging records queries answered locally, over the public internet, or forwarded to on-premises DNS servers via Resolver Endpoints. Users can share query logging configurations across multiple accounts using AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM). Query logs can be sent to Amazon S3, Amazon CloudWatch Logs, or Amazon Data Firehose. There is no additional charge for Route 53 Resolver Query Logging, but users may incur charges from the selected log storage services. For more information, visit the Route 53 Resolver product page or documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/09/amazon-route-53-resolver-query-logging-available-asia-pacific-nz/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"route53","skill_description":"Configures Amazon Route 53 DNS: public and private records, traffic-steering routing policies, health checks, DNS Firewall, Route 53 Profiles, VPC Resolver (also known as Route 53 Resolver) for hybrid and Outposts networks, and Global Resolver. Applicable when the customer wants to point a hostname at a target, split or fail over traffic across endpoints, monitor an endpoint, block malicious domains, centralize DNS across accounts, or resolve private DNS across a hybrid network. Routes to the right per-task procedure in references. Does not cover CloudFront-specific setup or non-DNS networking.\n\n\nServices: route53, vpc, outposts, cloudwatch, sns\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor, deploy\nPersona: developer, devops, network-admin, security-engineer, sre\nWorkload: networking, security, hybrid, disaster-recovery\n*","skill_name":"route53"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon Route 53 Resolver Query Logging now available in Asia Pacific (Taipei) - AWS","context":"Amazon Route 53 Resolver Query Logging is now available in the Asia Pacific (Taipei) region. This feature allows logging of DNS queries originating from Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC), enabling users to view queried domain names, AWS resources' sources (including IP and instance ID), and received responses. Route 53 Resolver, the Amazon DNS server in all VPCs, responds to queries for public DNS records, VPC-specific DNS names, and private hosted zones","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/07/amazon-route-53-resolver-query-logging-taipei/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-networking","skill_description":"Routes AWS networking requests to the correct service skill for implementation. Covers Route 53 (DNS, health checks, routing policies, Resolver, DNS Firewall), CloudFront (caching, edge, OAC, mTLS, signed URLs), Transit Gateway (multi-VPC hub, segmentation, centralized egress), Direct Connect (hybrid link, DX Gateway, MACsec), Site-to-Site VPN (IPsec tunnels, static or BGP), WAF (web ACLs, AWS Managed Rules, rate-based rules, Bot and Fraud Control), and Shield Advanced (L3/L4 DDoS). Applicable when creating, configuring, troubleshooting, or designing across these services, choosing between them, or diagnosing connectivity or traffic-filtering issues. Not for VPC subnets and route tables, load balancers, VPC endpoints, PrivateLink, API Gateway, IAM policy logic, container or serverless networking, or IaC authoring.\n\nServices: route53, route53resolver, cloudfront, directconnect, transitgateway, vpn, waf, shield\nTasks: configure, troubleshoot, debug, deploy, secure, migrate\nPersona: developer, network-engineer, devops, security-engineer\nWorkload: networking, security\n*","skill_name":"aws-networking"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"configuring-vpc-endpoints-for-private-aws-service-access","skill_description":"Configures VPC endpoints (interface and gateway) for private AWS service access using AWS PrivateLink. Use when setting up secure private connectivity to S3, DynamoDB, and other AWS services without internet gateway, NAT device, or public IP addresses. Covers endpoint creation, security groups, route tables, and DNS configuration.\n\nServices: ec2, vpc, s3, dynamodb\nTasks: configure\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: networking\n*","skill_name":"configuring-vpc-endpoints-for-private-aws-service-access"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify whether VPC Flow Logs capture DNS traffic to the VPC resolver"}
